<article class="env-theorem" id="09DW">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09DW">Theorem <span data-tag="09DW">9.21.7</span> <span class="named">(Fundamental theorem of Galois theory)</span>.</a>  Let $L/K$ be a finite Galois extension with Galois group $G$. Then we have $K = L^ G$ and the map </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \{ \text{subgroups of }G\}  \longrightarrow \{ \text{subextensions }L/M/K\} ,\quad H \longmapsto L^ H  \]
</div>
  <p> is a bijection whose inverse maps $M$ to $\text{Gal}(L/M)$. The normal subgroups $H$ of $G$ correspond exactly to those subextensions $M$ with $M/K$ Galois. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemma <a href="/tag/09I2" data-tag="09I2">9.21.4</a> given a subextension $L/M/K$ the extension $L/M$ is Galois. Of course $L/M$ is also finite (Lemma <a href="/tag/09G5" data-tag="09G5">9.7.3</a>). Thus $|\text{Gal}(L/M)| = [L : M]$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/09I1" data-tag="09I1">9.21.2</a>. Conversely, if $H \subset G$ is a finite subgroup, then $[L : L^ H] = |H|$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/09I3" data-tag="09I3">9.21.6</a>. It follows formally from these two observations that we obtain a bijective correspondence as in the theorem. 
    <p>
      If $H \subset G$ is normal, then $L^ H$ is fixed by the action of $G$ and we obtain a canonical map $G/H \to \text{Aut}(L^ H/K)$. This map has to be injective as $\text{Gal}(L/L^ H) = H$. Hence $|G/H| = [L^ H : K]$ and $L^ H$ is Galois by Lemma <a href="/tag/09I1" data-tag="09I1">9.21.2</a>. 
    <p>
      Conversely, assume that $K \subset M \subset L$ with $M/K$ Galois. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0BME" data-tag="0BME">9.15.7</a> we see that every element $\tau \in \text{Gal}(L/K)$ induces an element $\tau |_ M \in \text{Gal}(M/K)$. This induces a homomorphism of Galois groups $\text{Gal}(L/K) \to \text{Gal}(M/K)$ whose kernel is $H$. Thus $H$ is a normal subgroup. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>